Subject: [PATCH] Exercise the broken-pipe path after an asynchronous TCP reset

Darwin send() can first report ECONNRESET when a TCP peer closes. Consume that
pending reset and continue the existing bounded send loop until the intended
EPIPE/EPROTOTYPE path. Allow 1 ms between retries so kernel close processing is
not raced by 100 immediate buffered sends. Keep the socket-pair test, eventual
broken-pipe error and SIGPIPE handler assertions; production code is unchanged.

Reference: https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/System/Conceptual/ManPages_iPhoneOS/man2/send.2.html

--- a/android/android-emu-base/android/base/sockets/SocketUtils_unittest.cpp
+++ b/android/android-emu-base/android/base/sockets/SocketUtils_unittest.cpp
@@ -13,6 +13,9 @@
 
 #include "aemu/base/sockets/ScopedSocket.h"
 #include <gtest/gtest.h>
+
+#include <chrono>
+#include <thread>
 
 #include <errno.h>
 #include <signal.h>
@@ -201,8 +204,19 @@
         const int kMaxSendCount = 100;
         int n = 0;
         while (n < kMaxSendCount) {
+            if (n > 0) {
+                // Give the asynchronous TCP close/reset time to reach this
+                // endpoint instead of filling its send buffer in a tight loop.
+                std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(1));
+            }
             int ret = socketSend(s3.get(), "xxxx", 4);
             if (ret < 0) {
+                if (errno == ECONNRESET) {
+                    // Consume the pending reset and exercise EPIPE on a later
+                    // send. The signal handler checks every attempt below.
+                    ++n;
+                    continue;
+                }
 #ifdef __APPLE__
                 // On OS X, errno is sometimes EPROTOTYPE instead of EPIPE
                 // when this happens.
